<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Notation and omitted details</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we include some technical details concerning the theoretical results of the paper that were omitted for the sake of readability.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Properties of the forward process</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction: Diffusion, manifolds and generalisation ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we introduced the forward process in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 1 Introduction: Diffusion, manifolds and generalisation ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Throughout the proofs, we use the following property of the forward process:</p>
<table id="A1.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{t}|X_{0}\sim N(\mu_{t}X_{0},\sigma_{t}I_{d}),\qquad\mu_{t}=e^{-\alpha t},\qquad\sigma^{2}_{t}=\begin{cases}\alpha^{-1}(1-\mu_{t}^{2}),&amp;\text{if }\alpha&gt;0,\\
2t,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}|X_{0}\sim N(\mu_{t}X_{0},\sigma_{t}I_{d}),\qquad\mu_{t}=e^{-\alpha t},\qquad\sigma^{2}_{t}=\begin{cases}\alpha^{-1}(1-\mu_{t}^{2}),&amp;\text{if }\alpha&gt;0,\\
2t,&amp;\text{otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this follows immediately from properties of the Wiener process and when <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> becomes the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process and the result follows from a standard analysis (e.g. see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pavliotis [2014]</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using this fact, we have the following closed-form expression for the density <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the density of the empirical forward process <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\hat{p}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\hat{p}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\bigg(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{X_{t}|X_{0}}(x|x_{i})\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\bigg(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{X_{t}|X_{0}}(x|x_{i})\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\log\bigg(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(-\|x-\mu_{t}x_{i}\|^{2}/2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigg)-\frac{d}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma_{t}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\log\bigg(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(-\|x-\mu_{t}x_{i}\|^{2}/2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigg)-\frac{d}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma_{t}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\|x-\mu_{t}x_{i}\|^{2}/2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigg\}_{i=1}^{N}\bigg)+C_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>LSE</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\|x-\mu_{t}x_{i}\|^{2}/2\sigma_{t}^{2})\bigg\}_{i=1}^{N}\bigg)+C_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}=-\log(N)-\frac{d}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma_{t}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}=-\log(N)-\frac{d}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma_{t}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and we recall the definition of the function <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LSE}(\{r_{i}\}_{i}):=\log(\sum_{i}\exp(r_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LSE}(\{r_{i}\}_{i}):=\log(\sum_{i}\exp(r_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Rényi divergence</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a brief exposition of the Rényi divergence, which is a measure of difference between two measures. Given two measures <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu,\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu,\nu</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in(1,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in(1,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> we define the <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-Rényi divergence by</p>
<table id="A1.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{q-1}\log\int(\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}(x))^{q-1}\mu(dx),&amp;\text{ if }\mu\ll\nu,\\
\infty,&amp;\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=\begin{cases}\frac{1}{q-1}\log\int(\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}(x))^{q-1}\mu(dx),&amp;\text{ if }\mu\ll\nu,\\
\infty,&amp;\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For the case of <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=1</annotation></semantics></math> we set <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> to be the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{1}(\mu\|\nu)=\begin{cases}\int\log\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}(x)\ \mu(dx),&amp;\text{ if }\mu\ll\nu,\\
\infty,&amp;\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext> otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}(\mu\|\nu)=\begin{cases}\int\log\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}(x)\ \mu(dx),&amp;\text{ if }\mu\ll\nu,\\
\infty,&amp;\text{ otherwise.}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Indeed, whenever <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be shown that <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lim_{q\to 1^{+}}D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=D_{1}(\mu\|\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>lim</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{q\to 1^{+}}D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=D_{1}(\mu\|\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, whenever <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>-almost surely, we obtain,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lim_{q\to\infty}D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=\log\bigg(\operatorname{ess\,sup}_{\mu}\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}\bigg)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>ess</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>sup</mi></mrow><mi>μ</mi></msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{q\to\infty}D_{q}(\mu\|\nu)=\log\bigg(\operatorname{ess\,sup}_{\mu}\frac{d\mu}{d\nu}\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">which is taken to be <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\infty}(\mu\|\nu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\infty}(\mu\|\nu)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the Rényi divergence provides a natural interpolation between the KL divergence and the worst-case regret, with <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> increasing in <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. This measure of distance recently gained popularity in the sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vempala and Wibisono, 2019</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chewi et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erdogdu et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mousavi-Hosseini et al., 2023</a>]</cite> and privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mironov, 2017</a>]</cite> literatures as a stronger alternative to traditional divergences. We refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">van Erven and Harremoes, 2014</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chewi et al., 2022</a>]</cite> for further properties of this divergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Projections</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this work, we frequently utilise the projection mapping <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> which maps <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to the nearest element of <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In cases where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is curved, we run in to the issue that the projection is not well-defined as there could be multiple elements that are equally close to <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. In most places in the proof we consider quantities <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> that are sufficiently close that the projection function is uniquely defined (see reach in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Manifold reach ‣ Appendix C Lemmata ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>) but, for example, when we define the manifold adapted kernel in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 3.2 The case of curved manifolds ‣ 3 Geometry-adaptivity of log-domain smoothing ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), we use the projection for all <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the proofs of this work we do not utilise any property of the projection aside from the fact that it maps <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to some element of the manifold that is of distance <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(x,{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(x,{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math> away from <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. For that reason, <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be taken to be any mapping onto <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x-\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}(x)\|=\operatorname{dist}(x,{\mathcal{M}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x-\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}(x)\|=\operatorname{dist}(x,{\mathcal{M}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken to be a closed set, such a mapping always exists and we will take this choice of mapping to be fixed throughout the work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> the samples generated at early stopping time <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> are slightly biased due to contractions of the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process. For this reason, we will frequently consider the contracted manifold,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}{\mathcal{M}}=\{\mu_{\epsilon}x:x\in{\mathcal{M}}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}{\mathcal{M}}=\{\mu_{\epsilon}x:x\in{\mathcal{M}}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is as defined in (<a href="#A1.E13" title="Equation 13 ‣ A.1 Properties of the forward process ‣ Appendix A Notation and omitted details ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>), and we will frequently use the shorthand <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}:=\mu_{\epsilon}{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}:=\mu_{\epsilon}{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a projection mapping <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we take the projection mapping <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> to be given by</p>
<table id="A1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}}(x)=\mu_{\epsilon}\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}(x/\mu_{\epsilon})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{{\mathcal{M}}^{\epsilon}}(x)=\mu_{\epsilon}\Pi_{\mathcal{M}}(x/\mu_{\epsilon}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Manifold-adaptivity in the affine setting</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin with the proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Warm-up: linear setting ‣ 3 Geometry-adaptivity of log-domain smoothing ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> concerning the affine setting. Recall that we assume that the support of <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\operatorname{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>data</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\operatorname{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> is restricted to the affine subspace <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:Ax=b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}:Ax=b\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{*}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{*}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is row-orthonormal and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathbb{R}^{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we write the smoothing kernel in the following form:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{x}:=\operatorname{law}(x+\xi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>law</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{x}:=\operatorname{law}(x+\xi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> is a centred random variable independent of <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout the proof, we will use the null space projection matrix <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P:=I-A^{T}A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P:=I-A^{T}A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.1 Warm-up: linear setting ‣ 3 Geometry-adaptivity of log-domain smoothing ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection matrix onto <math id="A2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Null}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Null</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Null}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>, any <math id="A2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed as</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|z\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|z\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|(I-P)z\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|(I-P)z\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|A^{T}Az\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|A^{T}Az\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|Az\|^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\|Pz\|^{2}+\|Az\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the final line follows from the fact that <math id="A2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is row-orthonormal and so <math id="A2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AA^{T}=I_{d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AA^{T}=I_{d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Fact 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Warm-up: linear setting ‣ 3 Geometry-adaptivity of log-domain smoothing ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fact</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and the assumption that <math id="A2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ax_{i}=b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ax_{i}=b</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="A2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[N]</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the identity</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|z-x_{i}\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)+C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|z-x_{i}\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)+C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(z-x_{i})\|^{2}+\|A(z-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)+C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(z-x_{i})\|^{2}+\|A(z-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)+C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(z-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)-\frac{\|Az-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+C_{t}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(z-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)-\frac{\|Az-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+C_{t}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">This decomposition separates the influence of <math id="A2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> into normal and tangent directions with respect to <math id="A2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, let <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{x}=(x+\xi)\sim k_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{x}=(x+\xi)\sim k_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{Y}_{x}=(x+P\xi)\sim k_{x}^{{\mathcal{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{Y}_{x}=(x+P\xi)\sim k_{x}^{{\mathcal{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=P^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=P^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|=\|P(\tilde{Y}_{x}-x_{i})\|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|=\|P(\tilde{Y}_{x}-x_{i})\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore, using the fact that <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is centred, we also have,</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|AY_{x}-b\|^{2}]=\|Ax-b\|^{2}+\mathbb{E}[\|A\xi\|^{2}],\qquad\mathbb{E}[\|A\tilde{Y}_{x}-b\|^{2}]=\|Ax-b\|^{2}+\mathbb{E}[\|AP\xi\|^{2}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\|AY_{x}-b\|^{2}]=\|Ax-b\|^{2}+\mathbb{E}[\|A\xi\|^{2}],\qquad\mathbb{E}[\|A\tilde{Y}_{x}-b\|^{2}]=\|Ax-b\|^{2}+\mathbb{E}[\|AP\xi\|^{2}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">In particular, substituting into (<a href="#A2.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ Proof of Proposition . ‣ Appendix B Manifold-adaptivity in the affine setting ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) and taking the expectation, we conclude that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(Y_{x})]=\mathbb{E}\bigg[\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)\bigg]-\frac{\|Ax-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+C," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(Y_{x})]=\mathbb{E}\bigg[\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)\bigg]-\frac{\|Ax-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+C,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">and</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(\tilde{Y}_{x})]=\mathbb{E}\bigg[\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)\bigg]-\frac{\|Ax-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+\tilde{C}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>+</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\log\hat{p}_{\epsilon}(\tilde{Y}_{x})]=\mathbb{E}\bigg[\operatorname{LSE}\bigg(\bigg\{-\frac{\|P(Y_{x}-x_{i})\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}\bigg\}_{i}\bigg)\bigg]-\frac{\|Ax-b\|^{2}}{2\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}}+\tilde{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">for constants <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C,\tilde{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C,\tilde{C}</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the log-density of <math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{k}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{k}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}^{k^{\mathcal{M}}}_{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>ϵ</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{k^{\mathcal{M}}}_{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> are identical up to a constant.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Lemmata</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For proving the results for the more the general manifold setting, we require several additional properties of the log-sum-exp function, smooth manifolds and tubular neighbourhoods. In this section we collect these results.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Stability of the <math id="A3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LSE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>LSE</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LSE}</annotation></semantics></math> function</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma provides stability bounds for the LSE function which we make use of throughout our analysis. It can be seen as a generalisation of Fact <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Fact 3.2. ‣ 3.1 Warm-up: linear setting ‣ 3 Geometry-adaptivity of log-domain smoothing ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> which is heavily used in the analysis of the affine case.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma C.1</span></span><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\varepsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\subset\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\varepsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}\subset\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})\epsilon_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})}dr." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})=\int_{0}^{1}\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})\epsilon_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})}dr.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, we have that</span></p>
<table id="A3.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})\leq\max\{\epsilon_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N})\leq\max\{\epsilon_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">From the chain rule, we compute the partial derivatives,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i})=\frac{\exp(x_{j})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i})=\frac{\exp(x_{j})}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, by the fundamental theorem of calculus, we obtain that</span></p>
<table id="A3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\}_{i})-\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}\}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\epsilon_{j}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i}\}_{i})\,dr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>LSE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\epsilon_{j}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}\operatorname{LSE}(\{x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i}\}_{i})\,dr</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\frac{\exp(x_{j}+r\epsilon_{j})\epsilon_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})}dr," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\int_{0}^{1}\sum_{j=1}^{N}\frac{\exp(x_{j}+r\epsilon_{j})\epsilon_{j}}{\sum_{i=1}^{N}\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})}dr,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">completing the proof of (<a href="#A3.E17" title="Equation 17 ‣ Lemma C.1. ‣ C.1 Stability of the LSE function ‣ Appendix C Lemmata ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>). To obtain (<a href="#A3.E18" title="Equation 18 ‣ Lemma C.1. ‣ C.1 Stability of the LSE function ‣ Appendix C Lemmata ‣ Diffusion Models and the Manifold Hypothesis:Log-Domain Smoothing is Geometry Adaptive" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), we use the fact that the sum is a weighted average of the sequence <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon_{i}\}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon_{i}\}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so it has the property that,</span></p>
<table id="A3.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{\exp(x_{i}+r\epsilon_{i})\epsilon_{i}}{\sum_{j=1}^{N}\exp(x_{j}+r\epsilon_{j})}\leq\max_{i}\epsilon_{i}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mr